// error
// #5738: `bit check` accepted a call to a foreign class's unexported METHOD,
// contradicting SPEC §17.3 ("an exported type may still have unexported
// methods") and leaving the rule enforced for a FIELD (E0046,
// `vForeignFieldAccess`, #4631) with no counterpart for a method. `vMethodCall`
// (validatemethodcall.bit) resolved a call by signature alone
// (`methodSigLookup`) and never asked whether the resolved method was
// visible from here, so `t.bump()` on an unexported `bump` compiled, linked
// and ran across a module boundary.
//
// `std/json`'s `JsonDecodeError.message()` is the case this ticket traced
// the bug to: it satisfies the predeclared `error` interface, so a caller
// reaches it correctly through an `error` value (`e.message()`, an INTERFACE
// receiver, never judged here), but never through the concrete downcast
// type — `docs/stdlib/json.md` never told a caller to call `message()` that
// way, unlike `expected`/`found`, which ARE documented for direct field
// access after `e.(JsonDecodeError)` and are marked `export` for exactly
// that reason (#4631).
//
// Fixed in validatemethodcall.bit's `vForeignMethodCall`, mirroring
// `vForeignFieldAccess` byte for byte in code, message and hint: an
// unexported method and an unexported field are the same fault, a name the
// source module did not publish.
import { JsonDecodeCause, JsonDecodeError } from "std/json"

fn main() {
  let e = JsonDecodeError{ cause = JsonDecodeCause.MissingKey, path = "id", expected = "", found = "" }
  println(e.message())
}
